</style>

<main id="java-and-maven-builds-page" class="container py-5">

    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/Jenkins/Views/Shared/JenkinsTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- PAGE TITLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h1 class="fw-bold">Java and Maven Builds</h1>
                <p class="lead">
                    Learn how to configure Jenkins to compile, test, package, and automate Java applications using Apache Maven and the Jenkins Global Tool Configuration.
                </p>
            </div>

            <!-- INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">What is Java and Maven Integration in Jenkins?</h2>
                <p>
                    Java applications require a Java Development Kit (JDK) to compile code and a build automation tool like Apache Maven to manage dependencies, coordinate lifecycles, and package final outputs into JAR or WAR files.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Integrating Maven with Jenkins ensures that your application lifecycle—from standard compilations down to artifact deployments—runs deterministically inside an isolated CI/CD environment. This prevents local compilation quirks from slipping through to production environments.
                </p>
                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong> A production pattern that injects explicit versions of the JDK and Maven into automated pipeline environments to cleanly build, test, and package Java source code.
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- KEY CONCEPTS -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Key Concepts</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>POM Object Model (pom.xml)</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">The core project configuration file read by Maven that controls app dependencies, plug-in extensions, and target packaging parameters.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Maven Lifecycle Phases</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Standard target actions mapped to your pipeline steps such as <code>mvn clean</code>, <code>mvn test</code>, and <code>mvn package</code>.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Global Tool Configuration</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Defining JDK and Maven installations within the Jenkins controller settings so individual nodes can download and isolate runtimes on-demand.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <div class="info-card">
                            <h5>Dependency Caching</h5>
                            <p class="mb-0">Configuring agents to retain local <code>.m2/repository</code> caches to accelerate consecutive pipeline build iterations.</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <!-- EXAMPLE -->
            <div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practical Jenkins Example</h2>
                <p>This declarative pipeline demonstrates how to declare explicitly managed versions of JDK and Maven to run linting, unit tests, and code packaging steps:</p>
                <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any
    






















    tools {
        // Automatically configures predefined versions from Jenkins Global Tool Configurations
        jdk 'OpenJDK-17'
        maven 'Maven-3.9'
    }
    
    stages {
        stage('Initialize & Validate') {
            steps {
                echo 'Checking Runtime Environment...'
                sh 'java -version'
                sh 'mvn -version'
            }
        }
        
        stage('Execute Tests') {
            steps {
                echo 'Running unit test suite...'
                // Cleans old targets and runs tests defined in pom.xml
                sh 'mvn clean test'
            }
        }
        
        stage('Package Application') {
            steps {
                echo 'Compiling binaries into distribution formats...'
                // Skips unit tests to save execution overhead during packaging
                sh 'mvn package -DskipTests'
            }
        }
        

        stage('Archive Target Artifacts') {
            steps {
                echo 'Saving compiled JAR files to Jenkins Controller...'
                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar', fingerprint: true
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- PRACTICE -->
<div class="mb-4">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold">Practice Exercise</h2>
                <ol>
                    <li class="mb-2">Navigate to <strong>Manage Jenkins</strong> &rarr; <strong>Tools</strong> and verify your configured names under <strong>JDK</strong> and <strong>Maven</strong> match your script labels.</li>
                    <li class="mb-2">Create a new <strong>Pipeline</strong> job in Jenkins named <code>java-maven-build-pipeline</code>.</li>
                    <li class="mb-2">Paste the declarative pipeline script above, substituting your specific Java and Maven environment identifiers if necessary.</li>
                    <li class="mb-2">Run the build, open the <strong>Console Output</strong>, and review the standard Maven outputs downloading dependencies and creating target build artifacts.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>

            <!-- SUMMARY -->
            <div class="alert alert-info mt-4">
                <h2 class="h4 fw-bold">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">You have completed the <strong>Java and Maven Builds</strong> lesson. Knowing how to run standard Maven targets inside structured steps enables you to seamlessly manage advanced enterprise patterns like microservice frameworks.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- NAVIGATION -->
            <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
                <a class="btn btn-outline-primary" href="@Url.Action("Index", "LanguageStacks", new { area = "Jenkins" })">← Previous: Language Stack Pipelines</a>
                <a class="btn btn-primary" href="@Url.Action("SpringBoot", "LanguageStacks", new { area = "Jenkins" })">Next: Spring Boot CI Pipeline →</a>
            </div>

        </section>

    </div>

</main>
